# TDD Guide: C
## Why was this guide created so late
`cleanup/references/lang-matrix.md` lists **seven** languages whose quality this
repository judges: Go, Rust, Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Java, and C. For a
long time this skill's `langs/` directory had only six guides. One language was
judged without having authoring guidance, and the table explicitly recorded the
mismatch: "C is the seventh language, but has no guide." This file fills that gap.
There was a reason to postpone C. The other six languages have the same appearance of "choose a framework and write mocks," but **C is part of the linker in test design**, so the procedure differs.
## Detection
`CMakeLists.txt` · `Makefile` + `*.c` · `configure.ac` · `meson.build` one of them.
**`Makefile` Standalone is weak evidence** — Go and Rust projects also have Makefiles. `*.c`
Only judge as C when the source is present, and ask the user if C++(`*.cpp`) is mixed in.
## Framework
| | Unity | Google Test (gtest) | CMocka |
|--|--|--|--|
| Language | C only (C89 compatible) | C++ (C code testing possible) | C only |
| Size | 3 files — vendorable | Large build dependency | No dependency other than libc |
| mock | CMock (code generation) separate | Integrated in gmock | **Integrated** (`will_return`/`expect_value`) |
| Embedded | Primary use | Host build only | Possible |
**The basic is Unity + CMock.** If you put 3 source files into the repository, that's it, so the same tests run on the target board, and the introduction cost is the lowest in a C codebase where TDD is first applied.
**If you are already building a C++ project, gtest.** gmock comes with it, and you can verify the `assert()` and `abort()` paths with death tests (`EXPECT_DEATH`). However, the target C header must be wrapped by `extern "C"`.
If you plan to use mocks a lot, use CMocka. It is the only choice that can be mocked without code generation.
## File Structure
```
CMakeLists.txt
src/
checkout.c
checkout.h
pg_client.c # external payment gateway — replace at this boundary in tests
pg_client.h
tests/
unity/ # vendored Unity (unity.c/.h/internals.h)
test_checkout.c
mocks/
mock_pg_client.c # generated by CMock or handwritten test double
docs/tests/
TEST_PLAN.md
```
Make `src/` the library target and keep `main()` in a thin executable. If there is logic inside `main()`, it cannot be linked in tests — this is the most common cause of test failure in C.
## AAA Pattern
```c
#include "unity.h"
#include "checkout.h"
void test_checkout_applies_discount(void) {
/* Arrange */
checkout_t sut;
checkout_init(&sut, &fake_pg);
mock_pg_charge_result = PG_OK;
/* Act */
int paid = checkout_apply(&sut, 10000, "SALE10");
/* Assert */
TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL_INT(9000, paid);
TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL_INT(1, mock_pg_charge_calls);
}
```
`setUp()` / `tearDown()` calls Unity for each test. **You must initialize the global state here** — since C has no runtime to isolate tests, omitting initialization will result in order-dependent bugs (`references/antipatterns.md`).
## Boundary Replacement — Linker is Mock
In other languages, C is used for the DI position with three seams. Prefers from the top.
1. **Function Pointer Injection** — Place `int (*charge)(int)` in the structure and plug it in for testing. This is the simplest as it doesn't touch the linker, and it has the side effect of making the production code testable.
2. **Link-time Substitution** — Substitute `mock_pg_client.c` when linking the test binary. You don't need to modify the production code, but since it is at the file level, you cannot replace only a part of a single file's function.
3. **`--wrap`** — Call `gcc -Wl,--wrap=pg_charge` to redirect the call to `__wrap_pg_charge`. The original remains as `__real_pg_charge`. This is specific to GNU ld / lld and does not affect calls within the same translation unit, such as static functions.
## Execution Command
```bash
cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DENABLE_COVERAGE=ON
cmake --build build
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
ctest --test-dir build -R checkout # filter by name
ctest --test-dir build --rerun-failed
# Run sanitizers in separate builds (ASan and TSan conflict when enabled together)
cmake -S . -B build-asan -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="-fsanitize=address,undefined -g"
cmake -S . -B build-tsan -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="-fsanitize=thread -g"
```
**The C test suite that doesn't run ASan/UBSan runs only half the tests.** Tests that use cover and go over heap are common, and their flaws are found by the customer, not by CI.
## Coverage — gcov
```bash
# Enable instrumentation during compilation
gcc --coverage -O0 -g -c src/checkout.c -o build/checkout.o
ctest --test-dir build
gcov -b -c build/checkout.o # -b = branch coverage
lcov --capture --directory build --output-file coverage.info
genhtml coverage.info --output-directory htmlcov
gcovr --xml-pretty -o coverage.xml # Cobertura — diff-gate input
```
**In C, 80% line coverage is simultaneously too much and too little.** It is too
little because code with 100% line coverage can still pass a null-pointer
dereference or integer overflow: the line ran, but the condition did not branch.
The minimum useful unit is therefore a **branch (`gcov -b`)**, not a line. It is
too much when a project contains targets that cannot be instrumented by gcov,
such as bootloaders, interrupt handlers, or vendor BSPs. Enforcing a repository
percentage makes people add those files to exclusions instead of adding tests;
the number rises while the risk stays.
## Safety Related Code — MC/DC
Software that receives certification, such as aviation (DO-178C), automotive (ISO 26262), or medical (IEC 62304), requires **MC/DC** (Modified Condition/Decision Coverage), not just branch coverage. MC/DC is required for **Level A** (catastrophic failure) software according to DO-178C, while Level B requires decision coverage and Level C requires statement coverage.
MC/DC requires that if any condition of a compound condition **reverses the result on its own**, it must be required.
```c
if (a && b) { ... }
```
- Sentence coverage: Satisfied if true for 1 cases
- Branch coverage: Satisfied if true/false for 2 cases
- **MC/DC**: Both pairs where `a` alone changes the result and `b` alone changes the result are required — `(T,T)` `(F,T)` `(T,F)` 3 cases
The number of cases required for a judgment of N conditions is at least N+1 cases. Therefore, in code that requires MC/DC, **breaking down compound conditions itself is refactoring that reduces testing costs**.
Tooling: `gcov -b` does not measure MC/DC. Use GCC 14+
`-fcondition-coverage`, LLVM `-fcoverage-mcdc` (Clang 18+), or commercial tools
such as VectorCAST, LDRA, or Cantata. **Do not require MC/DC outside certification
work**; its cost exceeds its benefit.
## diff coverage — gate only for new code
C is the language where the gate absolutely collapses. Files that cannot be measured exist, and code from 20 years ago is mixed in. A gate over the whole % grows the exclusion list, not the tests. Use [the diff coverage gate](../../verify/SKILL.md#the-diff-coverage-gate), which looks only at the lines this change added.
Requires Node.js 20+.
```bash
git fetch origin main
ctest --test-dir build
gcovr --xml-pretty -o coverage.xml
uvx diff-cover coverage.xml --compare-branch=origin/main --fail-under=90
node ${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/tdd/scripts/test_gates.js diff-coverage \
--diff <(git diff -U0 "$(git merge-base origin/main HEAD)"...HEAD) \
--coverage coverage.xml --fail-under 90
```
The function `static inline` in the header is measured separately for each translation unit it includes. Since the same line has different coverage for each file, the maximum value is used when combining the reports — `lcov --add-tracefile` does this.
**If there are no executable new lines, it passes.** If a PR with only changes to header declarations, macros, or comments is dropped as 0%, the gate is immediately ignored.
## Anti-pattern
```c
// [X] logic in main() cannot be linked into tests
int main(void) { /* 200 lines */ }
// CORRECT: move logic to a library target and leave only argument parsing in main
// [X] global state not reset in setUp creates order dependence
static checkout_t g_sut;
// CORRECT: call memset in setUp() or use a local variable
// [X] judge tests with assert(); it disappears entirely in NDEBUG builds
assert(paid == 9000);
// CORRECT: TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL_INT(9000, paid);
// [X] ignore a return value
checkout_apply(&sut, 10000, "SALE10");
TEST_ASSERT_TRUE(1); // equivalent to having no assertion
// [X] do not test integer overflow
// Boundary values INT_MAX, INT_MIN, 0, and -1 belong to B in ZOMBIES
// [X] try to catch use-after-free with coverage
// gcov does not detect memory errors; an ASan build does
// [X] apply --wrap to a static function
// Calls in the same translation unit do not go through the linker, so it cannot intercept them
```
Anti-patterns unrelated to language (play-key dependence, order dependence, excessive mock) are handled by `references/antipatterns.md`. Read regardless of language detection results.
